Venetium 0028 - chrome_elf does not install its third-party-DLL blocking hook on Windows ARM32.

BATCH-ARM-FIX-1, his decision (2026-10-04, Q1 "skip the hook on ARM32"). The first device trip of the -rtdiv build (Surface 2,
Windows 10 build 15035 ARM32): 11 of 11 launches crashed ~2 s after start, 22 Crashpad dumps, all ACCESS_VIOLATION reading 0x4 in
chrome_elf.dll third_party_dlls::LogLoadAttempt (third_party_dlls/logs.cc:240), called from the hook (NewNtMapViewOfSection64) while
the OS loader maps chrome.dll's imports. The faulting thread is a Windows 10 parallel-loader worker (TEB SameTebFlags 0x2000
LoaderWorker) whose TEB ThreadLocalStoragePointer is NULL; the thread-safe-static guard of GetAllowedLog() reads implicit TLS there.
Microsoft's own ARM cl emits the same TLS sequence, so this is the platform's early-load context, not a toolchain defect
(TOOLCHAIN-ISSUES.md N10). The hook was only active once HKCU\Software\Venetium\BLBeacon\state became 1, which happens the first time
a run reaches PostBrowserStart - the earlier build never got that far - and venetium.exe's SignalChromeElf() re-arms the beacon before
chrome.dll loads, so its two-failure safety never tripped.
1 file:
- chrome/chrome_elf/chrome_elf_main.cc: DllMain's "if (third_party_dlls::LeaveSetupBeacon()) third_party_dlls::Init();" is under
  #if !defined(_M_ARM) (clang-cl and cl define _M_ARM for 32-bit ARM only). On ARM32 the DllMain half of the beacon (LeaveSetupBeacon)
  and the hook are skipped - ResetBeacon and the browser's BrowserBlocklistBeaconSetup still write the BLBeacon key, harmlessly;
  the rest of chrome_elf is unchanged. chrome.dll already copes with an uninitialised hook (the beacon-off state): DrainLog,
  RegisterLogNotification and IsThirdPartyInitialized see no log mutex and return empty / false.
Checked (STRUCTURAL): chrome_elf_main.obj (ARM) no longer references LeaveSetupBeacon or third_party_dlls::Init; relinked
chrome_elf.dll is ARMNT with ASLR/DEP/CFG, the same 27 exports and version strings, and 6 fewer kernel32 imports (CreateMutexW,
K32GetMappedFileNameW, ReadProcessMemory, UnmapViewOfFile, VirtualProtectEx, WriteProcessMemory - the hook's own); chrome.dll and
venetium.exe relink byte-identical. Device (his run, equivalent state): with BLBeacon state set to 0 the same build passed the old
crash point and showed its window - the next failure there is a different one.

diff --git a/chrome/chrome_elf/chrome_elf_main.cc b/chrome/chrome_elf/chrome_elf_main.cc
index 531723b..df1e4de 100644
--- a/chrome/chrome_elf/chrome_elf_main.cc
+++ b/chrome/chrome_elf/chrome_elf_main.cc
@@ -78,10 +78,20 @@ BOOL APIENTRY DllMain(HMODULE module, DWORD reason, LPVOID reserved) {
         // Disable third party extension points.
         elf_security::EarlyBrowserSecurity();
 
+#if !defined(_M_ARM)
         // Initialize the blocking of third-party DLLs if the initialization of
         // the safety beacon succeeds.
         if (third_party_dlls::LeaveSetupBeacon())
           third_party_dlls::Init();
+#else
+        // Venetium 0028: no third-party DLL blocking hook on Windows ARM32.
+        // The hook (NtMapViewOfSection) also runs on the OS loader's parallel
+        // worker threads, which on Windows 10 ARM32 (build 15035) have no
+        // implicit TLS (TEB ThreadLocalStoragePointer is null), and its
+        // thread-safe statics read TLS there and fault. Without the hook the
+        // browser runs as it does when the setup beacon is off: the log
+        // exports see no mutex and return empty.
+#endif
       } __except (elf_crash::GenerateCrashDump(GetExceptionInformation())) {
       }
     } else if (!install_static::IsCrashpadHandlerProcess()) {
